title: A Letter from Lenin
subtitle: Lenin writes to the German comrades.
new-page: true
tags: event
priority: -1
view-if: {!
if (typeof RG === 'undefined') { return false; }
if (!RG.lw && this.game.scenes.lw_lib) { this._runActions(this.game.scenes.lw_lib.onArrival); }
return !!(RG.lw && RG.lw.ready('m_lenin'));
!}
on-arrival: {!
if (!RG.lw) { this._runActions(this.game.scenes.lw_lib.onArrival); }
RG.lw.fire('m_lenin');
var rivals = RG.present().filter(function (f) { return f != Q.leading_faction; });
rivals.sort(function (a, b) { return RG.num(Q[b + '_strength']) - RG.num(Q[a + '_strength']); });
Q.lw_rival = rivals.length ? rivals[0] : 0;
Q.lw_rival_name = rivals.length ? RG.fname(rivals[0]) : 0;
!}

= A Letter from Lenin

The letter is handwritten in German on squared paper torn from a notebook, and it has come by courier through Riga. It is signed **Lenin**.

[? if lw_lead = 2 : He has read the Zentrale's resolutions and finds in them a great deal of revolutionary impatience and very little arithmetic. "Win the majority of the working class first," he writes. ?][? if lw_lead = 4 : A party that refuses to work in the unions and in parliament is not radical, he writes, but childish, and it leaves the workers to the reformists. ?][? if lw_lead = 1 or lw_lead = 3 : He praises the Zentrale's work in the factories, and then asks, in three underlined lines, whether patience has not become a habit. ?] He ends by asking for facts: wages, prices, and the strength of the unions district by district.

- @publish: Print the letter in Die Rote Fahne.
- @weapon: Quote it against the [+ lw_rival_name +].
- @reply: Answer him, and argue.

@publish
subtitle: Lenin's authority behind his advice, for the whole party to see. The current he criticises will not like it.
on-arrival: {!
RG.ecci('lenin', 8);
if (Q.lw_lead == 2) { RG.lw.lean('unionist', 2, 5); RG.lw.lean('offensive', 0, -6); }
else if (Q.lw_lead == 4) { RG.lw.lean('luxemburgist', 2, 4); RG.lw.lean('ultraleft', 0, -6); }
else { RG.lw.lean('offensive', 1, 4); Q.militancy += 3; }
RG.news('Lenin writes to the German workers', 2);
!}

The letter fills the front page, with a photograph of the author.

@weapon
subtitle: The [+ lw_rival_name +] are beaten with Lenin's name. Lenin dislikes being used.
view-if: lw_rival
on-arrival: {!
RG.lw.lean(Q.leading_faction, 1, 5);
RG.lw.lean(Q.lw_rival, -1, -8);
RG.ecci('lenin', -4);
!}

Quotations from the letter, cut to fit, appear in the Zentrale's circulars. The [+ lw_rival_name +] ask to see the whole letter, and are refused.

@reply
subtitle: The German party argues with Lenin as an equal. He is curious, not angry.
on-arrival: {!
Q.independence += 5;
RG.lw.lean(Q.leading_faction, 1, 4);
!}

Lenin writes back a month later, two lines in the margin of a newspaper cutting: "Perhaps. We shall see."
